WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:02.767
-Welcome back to "Late Night".
We're here with Pedro Pascal.

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.033
And you're recently
in this building.

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:05.701
You hosted "SNL". I thought
you were fantastic.

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:07.501
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:09.100
Thank you!

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.934
My God.

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:13.100
Oh, thank you.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.501
-There were a couple of things
I want to ask you about.

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:19.634
First of all, I was so tickled
by the sketch where you woke up

00:00:19.634 --> 00:00:22.067
in a coma -- from a coma --

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.300
and you had a very distinct
voice, and, you know,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.100
every now and then, I reach out,
and I will say to, you know,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.267
a writer I know there,
"Who came up with that?"

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:31.701
And I was told that you showed
up with that voice,

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:35.167
which it's very funny to me
that you had cultivated

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.400
that voice outside
of sketch comedy.

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:41.300
-[ Laughs ]
-It seems -- Was that

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.901
your original voice
for "The Mandalorian",

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:44.167
and they were like, "No"?

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.267
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:47.267
[ Imitating L.A. accent ]
What are you talking about?

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:48.934
It's perfect.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:53.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:54.901
This is the way.

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:56.400
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.634
-[ Normal voice ] Yeah, I did.

00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:01.067
How dare they actually use
your ideas?

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.601
-Well, there's a really funny
thing that happens

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:05.200
where hosts show up and you'll
say, like, "You have any things?

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.267
You have any funny voices?

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.801
Do you do any impressions?"

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.167
And, I mean, the greatest thing
is when a host brings something

00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:12.133
like that and it sparks
something.

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.634
-Right.

00:01:13.634 --> 00:01:16.200
Yeah, we would talk like that
on the set of "The Last of Us".

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.467
It started with --
-Which I guess you have to do

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:20.968
to, like, deal with
what you're processing.

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:22.300
-Basically, yeah.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.634
Deal with somebody dying
every episode in front of you.

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:27.634
-Now I'm gonna watch
"The Last of Us" with my wife,

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:28.667
and at the end of every episode,
I'm gonna be like,

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:29.767
[ Imitating L.A. accent ]
That was so sad.

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.067
-[ Imitating L.A. accent ]
Oh, my God.

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.968
[ Laughs ] Exactly.
-Those monsters mean business.

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:35.234
-No kidding.

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:37.667
I can't believe he died.

00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:39.400
-He was, like, my favorite one!

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.734
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.067
-I don't know, what's gonna
happen next?

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.434
[ Laughs ]

00:01:43.434 --> 00:01:44.968
[ Normal voice ] I'm telling
you, it's very contagious.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:46.334
-[ Normal voice ]
Very contagious.

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:47.501
I got a little taste.

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:49.334
This is my breakfast club
now that I have it.

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:50.968
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:53.667
It will not stop,
it spread like a fungus

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:54.868
amongst the set.

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.501
-Yeah, by the way,
could you have, like,

00:01:56.501 --> 00:01:58.934
a mushroom pasta now
after "The Last of Us",

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.300
like, could you?
-Yeah, I love mushrooms.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.567
-Okay.
-All kinds.

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:03.634
-It hasn't put you --
Oh, all kinds?

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.767
-Yeah.
-Like the kind at restaurants

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:07.734
or the kinds you have to, like,
work a little harder to get?

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:09.467
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:11.234
The kinds in restaurants,

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:13.968
the kinds that used to come in,
like, a little Ziploc bag

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:16.667
in the '90s, and now the kinds
that are in these, like,

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.534
colorful chocolates.
-Yeah.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:20.133
I will tell you this --
I wouldn't want to have

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:22.601
one of those kinds and then
watch "The Last of Us".

00:02:22.601 --> 00:02:24.534
-That I would not do.

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:27.367
That might put you off it
for good

00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:28.667
so don't do that. [ Chuckles ]

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:30.501
-Are you -- I mean, obviously,
you knew you were making

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:31.767
a special show.

00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:34.467
Are you surprised, taken aback
by how, you know,

00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:35.968
this has become
one of these event shows

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:37.200
that people want to watch
when it comes out

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.367
so they can talk about it?

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:39.868
-I knew the game was
a really big deal.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:45.801
And I was really scared and just
presumed that the adaptation

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:49.968
wouldn't work, not because it's
been tricky adapting video games

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:53.300
into television shows or movies
or anything like that,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.167
but because, I don't know,
everyone loved it so much.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:58.734
When I saw all
of the story illustrations

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:00.567
for "The Mandalorian", I was
like, "This is gonna be a hit."

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:01.567
-Yeah.

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:02.801
-No worries.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:04.868
But, I guess, in this one,
you have to see my face.

00:03:04.868 --> 00:03:06.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.868 --> 00:03:11.834
-I know some people were --
-And there's no...

00:03:11.834 --> 00:03:13.834
-I like the idea
that your agent's like,

00:03:13.834 --> 00:03:15.767
"He would love to wear a helmet
in this."

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:17.033
-[ Laughs ] Exactly.

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:18.601
It works, it works!

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:20.467
-You know, obviously, big fans
of the video game,

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:24.033
and, I don't know,
I feel like it's very well-cast

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:28.033
where people --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:32.067
-Dude, he's hot.
-Yeah.

00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:33.334
I know, I think they're
both hot.

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:36.834
I think you did a good job.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:38.334
-All right.

00:03:38.334 --> 00:03:40.267
The show's great, and I'm gonna
circle back to one last thing

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:42.667
on "SNL", 'cause I do really
love when -- especially

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.467
when you can tell -- you can
tell the cast likes the host,

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:48.434
because the host
is sort of with the cast

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:50.434
at a moment where everybody
sort of gets the giggles,

00:03:50.434 --> 00:03:52.934
and there was this sketch --
"Lisa from Temecula" --

00:03:52.934 --> 00:03:56.100
where a girl was having trouble
cutting a steak,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.701
and the whole table was shaking.

00:03:57.701 --> 00:04:01.501
And you seemed surprised
by what was happening.

00:04:01.501 --> 00:04:03.701
Now, of course, I know, and I
think everybody at this point

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:05.000
knows there was
a dress rehearsal.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.534
Was it markedly different
on air?

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:09.968
-Yeah, it bombed during dress.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.267
There was a big bone
in the middle of the steak

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:14.067
that she couldn't saw through,
for one,

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:16.968
and they were just being
a little tame

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:19.334
about the table moving.

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:21.334
And it really didn't work.

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:24.501
And we said, "Oh, no, please
don't cut it.

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:28.367
If you really, really shake
the table

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:31.167
and you give her a steak that
she can saw through,"

00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:34.167
which ended up in her lap, and
the whole table --

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:36.033
It just kind of started
to happen.

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:38.400
We felt the note suddenly occur.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.667
And it just --
we all fell apart.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.868
It was really --
Bowen started it.

00:04:42.868 --> 00:04:44.634
-Yeah.
-And everyone says,

00:04:44.634 --> 00:04:46.100
"He never breaks."
-You just threw him

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.534
right under the bus right then,
yeah.

00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:49.400
-Sorry, Bowen.
-That's all right.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.067
I mean, I will say that was
a real moment

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.367
where it cut to the wide,
and Bowen seemed to have no idea

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:55.400
it was in the wide, 'cause he
wasn't even trying not to laugh.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.300
-He tossed his fork
and fully gave up.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.334
He was like, "I can't,"
you know?

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:02.567
It was over.

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:04.734
And when it was on me
and I was breaking,

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:06.200
it's because I was,
like, turning

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.100
every now and then, and -- yeah,
I'm throwing him under the bus,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.667
basically.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.667 --> 00:05:12.167
-'Cause I'm like, "No, no,
you never break"

00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:13.534
and so we were done for.

00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:15.167
-It was really fun to watch.

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:17.334
It was a really special episode.

00:05:17.334 --> 00:05:18.934
And, my goodness, it's so
wonderful to have you back.

00:05:18.934 --> 00:05:20.267
-It's so nice to see you again.

00:05:20.267 --> 00:05:21.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:21.434 --> 00:05:23.267
-You guys, how about that?
Pedro Pascal.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:27.300
New episodes of "The Last of Us"
air Sundays at 9

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.534
on HBO and HBO Max.

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:29.734
Third season of
"The Mandalorian"

00:05:29.734 --> 00:05:31.501
premiers on Disney+
Wednesday, March 1st.

00:05:31.501 --> 00:05:32.801
We'll be right back
with Taye Diggs.

